window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Build AI workflows in minutes, not hours","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html","text":"SPL is a compact Python-like language designed for fast iteration: keep deterministic code separate from LLM calls, contain model variance with explicit guards, and read the whole workflow at a glance. Spend time developing the solution, not wiring an orchestration layer. Write a small script, run it, inspect the result, and refine the prompt or guard. The goal is a working solution in minutes rather than hours of glue code. The ? is the only model call. fmt checks its output type; assert rejects an out-of-range answer before ordinary code routes it. A reviewer can see the input, the variable part, the guard, and the result without tracing through callbacks. These checks constrain the answer; they do not prove the severity assessment is correct.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#why-spl","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Design tenets","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#design-tenets","text":"enforce, forbid and len provide prompt guidance, not validation. Use schema and assert to validate results, and restrict to check actions.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#how-a-run-works","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Where to next","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#where-to-next","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#non-goals","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","text":"Go from an idea to a small, guarded workflow with a short edit-run-check loop. SPL keeps deterministic logic, model calls, and validation visible in one readable script, so you can iterate on the solution rather than build orchestration glue. Start with exact raw tools, add schema-validated generation, and opt into agents only when the model should choose actions. Syntax checks, scripts without LLM commands and recorded replay need no Copilot login.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Requirements","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#requirements","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Quick test","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#quick-test","text":"Create hello.spl: Check the syntax, log in and run it: The ? is the only model call; its result must be an integer. The assertion checks the allowed range, then ordinary code chooses a route. repairs=1 can correct one format failure, not a failed assertion. Use --model \u003cid> to choose a model instead of the SDK default. stdout vs. stderr The value passed to success or a top-level return goes to stdout: strings are printed raw, everything else as JSON. An uncaught failure prints its payload to stdout. Logs, including the model and reasoning effort of each LLM call, go to stderr so results can be piped safely.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"A short iteration loop","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#a-short-iteration-loop","text":"Replay never starts Copilot or MCP, but explicit script tools and stores still run. It does not repeat or restore agent-owned actions. Protect traces as sensitive data; see workflow replay.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Install & build","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install--build","text":"For a global installation: From a checkout, the interpreter lives in lang/: npm install also runs prepare, compiling TypeScript to dist/. The package exposes two equivalent binaries, spl and spl-lang, pointing at dist/cli.js.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Your first run","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#your-first-run","text":"From lang/ in a checkout: Check the syntax without model calls or script side effects: Log in to Copilot. When authentication is needed, GitHub's device flow prints a code and URL to confirm in the browser: Run against Copilot: Alternatively, from the repository root, use the local package with npx:","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Write a script","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#write-a-script","text":"For structured output, create user.spl: Literal braces in prompts Every non-raw string interpolates {expr}. For literal braces, double them ({{ }}), escape them (\\{ \\}), or use a raw string such as r'{\"a\": 1}'.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Exit codes","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#exit-codes","text":"Code Meaning --- --- 0 The script finished: it reached success , a top-level return or the end. 1 Uncaught failure; the failure payload is printed to stdout. 2 Syntax error or command-line usage error. 70 Internal interpreter error.","group":"Start here","signatures":["0","1","2","70"]},{"title":"Replay generation offline","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#replay-generation-offline","text":"Replay needs no model credentials. Requests must match exactly and in order. Workflow replay returns recorded generation and agent results without starting Copilot or MCP. Explicit script tools and stores still execute normally; agent-owned actions are not repeated. Traces contain prompts and raw responses; protect them as sensitive data. Legacy --record files retain generation-only replay restrictions. See trace/replay.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Breaking changes in 0.3","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#breaking-changes-in-03","text":"Old New --- --- read_file{path='a.txt'} 'Return it' read_file('a.txt') ; interpret the result with a separate generation call. Tools with missing parameters Required positional arguments, or explicit agent '...' for model-selected actions. instruct / avoid Use enforce / forbid for scoped prompt guidance. think{level='high'} ?{reasoning='high'} or think{reasoning='high'} . let x += 1 , let d.key = 2 x += 1 , d.key = 2 ; let declares or redeclares a name. Blocks share the function/script scope Each block creates a lexical child scope. Use assignment, not inner let , to update an enclosing declaration. Modifier values checked by parsing Values are expressions checked at runtime; unknown or duplicate names remain syntax errors.","group":"Start here","signatures":["read_file{path='a.txt'} 'Return it'","instruct","avoid","think{level='high'}","let x += 1","let d.key = 2"]},{"title":"Repository tour","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#repository-tour","text":"Path Contents --- --- [LANG-PRD.md](../LANG-PRD.md) Product requirements and language specification. [lang/](../lang/) The interpreter: src/ , tests/ , examples/ and docs/syntax.ebnf . [vscode/](../vscode/) VS Code extension: language server, TextMate grammar, snippets and tests. [docs/](../docs/) The HTML documentation site. [docs-template/](Introduction.md) Markdown documentation, starting with the introduction.","group":"Start here","signatures":[]},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#run-the-tests","text":"The default suite builds and runs offline, without model calls. It checks example syntax and documentation snippets. For authenticated live checks, explicitly set SPL_LIVE_TESTS=1. In PowerShell, run $env:SPL_LIVE_TESTS='1'; npm test, then remove the environment variable with Remove-Item Env:SPL_LIVE_TESTS.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Language basics","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html","text":"The deterministic core of SPL: Python-flavoured expressions, braces, lexical block scopes, and explicit assignment. Model calls are distinct expressions, not implicit behavior of ordinary functions. Read a script by its boundaries: let names values, ? and think mark model judgment, agent opts into model-selected actions, and assert exposes acceptance rules. Keep routine computation in ordinary code so the variable parts and their guards remain easy to spot. Design decisions at a glance Blocks use braces; the opening { must be on the same line as its keyword. Statements end at a newline (or ;). let declares/redeclares a local; x = … and x += … update an existing local. Collection assignment uses d['k'] = 2, without let. Block scoping: each block has a child scope. Reads and assignments search outward; let shadows enclosing names. See Scope. return exits immediately from any scope — the function, or the whole script at the top level. Every string interpolates {expr}. Unknown/duplicate modifier names, break outside loops, and try without except are syntax errors. Modifier values are expressions validated at runtime.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Program structure","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#program-structure","text":"A script is an optional @{…} directive header followed by statements. # starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Directives","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#directives","text":"The header directive is model, whose value must be a plain string without interpolation. Precedence is CLI --model, per-call model, header, then SDK default.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Variables","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#variables","text":"let name = expression declares or redeclares a local. Assignment and compound operators (+= -= *= /= //= %=) update existing locals; they do not use let. Member/item targets also omit let. Unknown reads and assignment to an undeclared local are catchable runtime errors.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Runtime inputs: $env and $args","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#runtime-inputs-env-and-args","text":"These special variables are available in every scope, including functions and string interpolation. They are snapshots taken at the start of each run, not ordinary bindings. The special names cannot be declared, rebound, or used as parameters or loop variables. Assignments to their members/items and mutating methods raise catchable runtime_error, even through aliases such as let args = $args. Use $args.copy(), list($args), $args[:], or $env.copy() to create a normal mutable copy. Copies have ordinary missing-index/key errors; this does not change ordinary dict/list behavior. The runtime does not automatically add either value to a variable store. Explicitly assigning a value or alias to a top-level variable can persist it, so do not persist, log, or interpolate sensitive environment values into prompts unintentionally. Replay uses the current run's inputs, not recorded environment variables or arguments. Other $-prefixed variable names are syntax errors.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Scope","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#scope","text":"let name = … binds in the current scope. Every block creates a lexical child scope. Inner declarations shadow enclosing names and cannot be accessed from a parent scope. Reads and assignments search outward through the full lexical chain for the first declaration. There is no hoisting: a local appears when execution binds it. Conditionals, loops, exception handlers, and guidance/restriction blocks follow the same rule. Each loop iteration has fresh locals, including its loop variables. Caught failure names belong to their handler. To keep a result, declare it outside and assign inside: enforce provides positive scoped prompt guidance and forbid provides negative guidance. Both create child variable scopes, but neither verifies model compliance. Functions close over their lexical defining scope. They can read or assign enclosing declarations; let instead declares locally and shadows an enclosing name. Lists and dicts remain mutable across scopes.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Block and conditional expressions","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#block-and-conditional-expressions","text":"A block or selected if/elif/else branch yields its final expression. An empty branch, a block ending in a statement, or an unmatched conditional returns None. Only the selected branch runs. {} remains an empty dictionary; use { pass } for a block yielding None. Functions still require explicit return.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Persistence","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#persistence","text":"Top-level variables can be saved between runs with opt-in --store, even after a script failure. Persistence is not a transaction or an action log. It is allowed during workflow trace replay, but forbidden during legacy generation replay. See CLI & runtime.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Types & values","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#types--values","text":"type() Literals Notes --- --- --- 'None' None Falsy. 'bool' True False Python spelling. 'number' 42 3.14 1e-3 One numeric type. int() truncates, float() parses. 'str' 'a' \"a\" '''a''' r'a' Indexed by Unicode character. 'list' [1, 2, 3] Mutable, trailing comma allowed. 'dict' {'a': 1} Keys must be strings. Insertion ordered. Also readable as d.a . 'function' def f(x) { … } First-class values. Model calls can request typed output with fmt='integer', 'float', 'array', 'object', 'text', or 'boolean'. Non-text formats validate strict JSON and return ordinary SPL values; fmt='json' accepts any JSON value. See output formats and examples. type() returns a string Compare it with a quoted name: type(x) == 'list'. There is no separate int/float distinction — both report 'number'.","group":"The language","signatures":["'None'","'bool'","'number'","'str'","'list'","'dict'","'function'"]},{"title":"Truthiness","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#truthiness","text":"None, False, 0, empty strings, empty lists and empty dicts are falsy. Everything else (including functions) is truthy.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Strings & interpolation","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#strings--interpolation","text":"All four quote styles interpolate {expression}. Any expression is allowed, including calls and indexing. Substitution is as is: strings raw, True/False/None in Python spelling, lists and dicts as JSON.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Quote styles & escapes","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#quote-styles--escapes","text":"Form Behaviour --- --- '…' \"…\" Single line; escapes and interpolation. '''…''' \"\"\"…\"\"\" May span lines; escapes and interpolation. Ideal for prompts. r'…' Raw: no escapes, no interpolation. Valid escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\' \\\" \\{ \\}. Anything else is a syntax error, and so is a stray single } in a string — write }} or \\}.","group":"The language","signatures":["'…'","\"…\"","'''…'''","\"\"\"…\"\"\"","r'…'"]},{"title":"Operators","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#operators","text":"Lowest to highest precedence: Operators Description --- --- x if cond else y Conditional expression. or Logical or (short-circuit). and Logical and (short-circuit). not Logical negation. == != \u003c \u003c= > >= in not in Comparison and membership. == is deep for lists and dicts. Ordering works on numbers, strings and lists. + - Add / subtract. + also joins two strings or two lists — mixing types is an error (use interpolation or str() ). * / // % Multiply, divide, floor-divide, modulo. * repeats strings and lists. Division by zero is a failure. -x +x Unary sign. ** Power. f(x) a[i] a[i:j] a.b Call, index, slice, member. Negative indexes count from the end.","group":"The language","signatures":["x if cond else y","or","and","not","== != \u003c \u003c= > >= in","not in","+ -","* / // %","-x +x","**","f(x)","a[i]","a[i:j]","a.b"]},{"title":"Control flow","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#control-flow","text":"","group":"The language"},{"title":"if / elif / else","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#if--elif--else","text":"","group":"The language"},{"title":"for & while","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#for--while","text":"for iterates lists, strings (characters) and dicts (keys). Several names unpack list items, which pairs well with items() and enumerate(). break, continue and pass work as in Python.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#functions","text":"Define with def. Functions take positional arguments only (the count is checked), return None unless return is used, can recurse (up to a maximum call depth of 200) and close over their defining scope. return leaves the innermost function immediately, from inside any nested block, loop, try or constraint block; at the top level of a script it ends the script like success, optionally with a value. Functions are the right place for validators — the deterministic code that decides whether to trust an LLM answer. See commit-message.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Built-ins","page":"Language basics","href":"language.html#built-ins","text":"A compact standard library is available without imports: len, range, sorted, json_parse, log, and Python-style methods on strings, lists and dicts. The full list is on the Built-ins page.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Generation, agents & tools","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html","text":"Keep deterministic logic in ordinary code and put LLM judgment at visible boundaries. Exact I/O, generation, and model-directed actions are distinct operations, so you can iterate on a prompt or guard without hiding which parts can vary or perform actions. Use fmt and schema to constrain returned values, assert for acceptance rules, and bounded repairs for format/schema corrections. These guards contain variance; they do not make the model deterministic or establish factual correctness.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Generation: ?","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#generation-","text":"? generates a value without runtime tools, built-in Copilot tools, or MCP access. It is a prefix operator: ?'a' + b means (?'a') + b. Pass external information explicitly: Each call starts a fresh session; previous answers are not implicit conversation history. Generated text is trimmed; raw tool output is not.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Composable modifiers","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#composable-modifiers","text":"?, think, and agent share these modifiers. Braces are optional. Values are expressions evaluated once; names must be known and unique. Invalid names are syntax errors; invalid values are catchable runtime errors before the model call. Modifier Values Meaning --- --- --- len 'word' , 'sentence' , 'paragraph' , 'long' Prompt hint, not a hard length limit. fmt 'integer' , 'float' , 'array' , 'object' , 'text' , 'boolean' , 'json' Text by default; JSON when a schema is supplied and no explicit format is given. reasoning 'low' , 'medium' , 'high' Requested model reasoning effort. Unsupported effort logs a warning and uses the model default. show True , False (default) Log available model reasoning at info level. schema JSON Schema dict or boolean Validate the result; implies JSON output. repairs Integer 0..5 , default 0 Additional generation attempts for format/schema errors only. Agents require zero. model Nonempty string Per-call model; CLI override wins, then this modifier, then the header, then the SDK default.","group":"The language","signatures":["len","fmt","reasoning","show","schema","repairs","model"]},{"title":"Validated structured output","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#validated-structured-output","text":"Schemas use synchronous JSON Schema draft-07 with Ajv strict mode. Types, enums, nested properties, required fields, arrays, numeric/string limits, and standard synchronous composition are supported. Unknown keywords, invalid schemas, unresolved remote references, and asynchronous schemas fail before generation. There is no coercion, default insertion, or removal of extra fields. Custom formats/plugins are not installed. A mismatch raises validation_failed with field paths, for example lines[2].quantity: must be number. Missing fields and additional properties also identify the offending path. Schemas validate structure; ordinary assert statements validate business rules.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Output formats","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#output-formats","text":"Format Required response SPL value --- --- --- 'integer' A JSON number with no fractional part, such as 55 . number 'float' Any finite JSON number, such as 3.14 or 42 . number 'array' A JSON array, such as [1, 2, 3] . list 'object' A JSON object, such as {\"ok\": true} . Arrays are rejected. dict 'text' Plain text, trimmed without JSON parsing. This is the default. str 'boolean' JSON true or false , not quoted strings or Python spelling. bool 'json' Any JSON value, including scalars and null . Parsed value Non-text formats use strict JSON: neither prose nor Markdown fences are extracted, and values are never coerced. Malformed output or the wrong type raises invalid_format and can be corrected with repairs. An explicit format is checked before any supplied schema. A schema cannot be combined with fmt='text'. Migration: fmt='object' is now object-only. Scripts expecting lists should use fmt='array', or fmt='json' to accept any JSON value.","group":"The language","signatures":["'integer'","'float'","'array'","'object'","'text'","'boolean'","'json'"]},{"title":"Bounded repair, not action retry","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#bounded-repair-not-action-retry","text":"repairs=2 permits at most three model calls: the initial call plus two corrections. Only invalid_format and validation_failed trigger correction. The next prompt contains the prior answer and validation errors. Prompt/modifier expressions are not reevaluated. Authentication, timeout, transport, tool, and restriction errors are not repaired; assertions outside the call are not repaired either. Generation has no tools, so repair cannot repeat writes or commands. Agents reject nonzero repairs, even when they also validate their final answer against a schema.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Think shorthand","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#think-shorthand","text":"think is generation-only shorthand with default medium reasoning. It accepts exactly the same modifiers as ?, so reasoning and structured output no longer require two separate calls.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Explicit agents","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#explicit-agents","text":"agent enables model-directed runtime tools and configured MCP tools. It defaults to medium reasoning. Restrict its capabilities deliberately; raw tools and agents are allow-by-default outside restrictions. Only agents expose tools to Copilot. Any permission request during generation is rejected and aborts the call. Agent tools still use the runtime restriction checks; unconfigured MCP servers and other Copilot tools are not approved. Agent actions are not transactional. --trace captures agent results and failures; replay returns them without repeating agent-owned actions. Legacy --record does not support agents.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Scoped guidance: enforce & forbid","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#scoped-guidance-enforce--forbid","text":"enforce adds positive guidance and forbid adds negative guidance for every model call inside the block. These are the language's scoped prompt-guidance keywords. Guidance applies dynamically to calls made inside the block, including called functions, and unwinds afterward. Each block has a child variable scope, so declare answer outside and assign inside when it must remain visible. Guidance is not validation enforce, forbid, and len influence a prompt; they do not verify compliance. Use schemas and assertions for output guarantees, and restrict for runtime action checks.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Exact raw tools","page":"Generation & agents","href":"llm-commands.html#exact-raw-tools","text":"Function Result Restriction --- --- --- read_file(path) Exact UTF-8 contents fs write_file(path, content) Write confirmation text fs web_fetch(url) HTTP response body text net web_search(query) Search result text net shell(command) Combined stdout/stderr; nonzero exit raises tool\\_error exec, plus inspected fs/net arguments Arguments are positional, required strings. These functions never invoke a model, never ask it to fill missing arguments, and do not truncate raw results. Agent-facing tool text may be truncated with an explicit marker. Network and shell operations retain their normal external variability; raw means no model interpretation. Workflow trace replay still executes these explicit raw tools and persistent stores under normal restrictions, but does not repeat agent-owned actions. The older generation record/replay mode permits local reads only and blocks writes, network, shell, agents, and persistent stores.","group":"The language","signatures":["read_file(path)","write_file(path, content)","web_fetch(url)","web_search(query)","shell(command)"]},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html","text":"restrict narrows raw tools and explicit agent actions. Outside restrictions they are allow-by-default. Generation has no tools at all, and replay further restricts execution to local reads and recorded generation.","group":"The language"},{"title":"The four forms","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#the-four-forms","text":"Form Effect --- --- restrict './target/out' { … } Reads and writes are allowed only inside the folder. Same as restrict fs . restrict net 'api.example.com' { … } Network access is allowed only to the listed hosts. restrict exec 'npm,git' { … } Shell commands are allowed only for the listed executables. restrict mcp 'docs/search,issues' { … } Only the named MCP tools or servers may be called. Servers must be configured with --mcp . The argument is an expression evaluating to a comma-separated string ('npm,git') or a list of strings. An empty list is an error. Combine clauses with commas. Each clause defaults to fs if its kind is omitted. All clauses apply together; repeated kinds intersect just like nested blocks. Commas inside strings or lists remain part of that clause's allowlist.","group":"The language","signatures":["restrict './target/out' { … }","restrict net 'api.example.com' { … }","restrict exec 'npm,git' { … }","restrict mcp 'docs/search,issues' { … }"]},{"title":"Two layers of enforcement","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#two-layers-of-enforcement","text":"1 · Guidance The runtime tells the model about the active restrictions in the prompt, so it plans within them. 2 · Hard enforcement The runtime intercepts every file, network and exec call and blocks any that violate the active scopes. A blocked call raises a restriction_violation failure. It is catchable with try/except:","group":"The language"},{"title":"Nesting","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#nesting","text":"Restrictions are scoped to their block and can be nested. Nested restrictions of the same kind intersect: they can only narrow the allowed set, never widen it. Different kinds combine freely.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Matching rules","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#matching-rules","text":"","group":"The language"},{"title":"File system","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#file-system","text":"Paths are canonicalized before checking: made absolute relative to --cwd, .. removed, symbolic links resolved — including dangling links, whose targets are followed so a write cannot escape through them. A path is allowed when it is the allowed folder itself or inside it. On Windows the comparison is case-insensitive.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Network","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#network","text":"net matches exact hostnames and *.example.com wildcards. Entries may be bare hosts or URLs (the host is extracted; ports are ignored). Only http: and https: URLs are fetched. Redirects are followed manually (at most 5) and the host is re-checked on every hop. web_search is network access too: it queries html.duckduckgo.com, so a restrict net scope must list that host for searches to work.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Executables","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#executables","text":"exec matches the executable name. On Windows .exe, .cmd, .bat and .com suffixes are ignored. Inside a restrict exec block, commands must be named without a path (git, not /usr/bin/git). Shell commands in pipelines and chains are checked against active restrictions: executables are checked against restrict exec, and path-like/URL arguments and redirection targets are checked against active fs and net scopes. Best effort for programs with their own I/O The runtime can only inspect what it sees on the command line. A program that touches files or the network on its own (a build tool, a package manager) is limited by its executable name, not by what it does internally.","group":"The language"},{"title":"MCP servers and tools","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#mcp-servers-and-tools","text":"Load servers with --mcp. A selector such as docs or docs/* allows every configured tool from that server; docs/search allows only its search tool. Names are case-sensitive. Nested scopes intersect, and cannot widen the configuration's optional tools allowlist. Disallowed servers and tools are removed before creating each LLM session. Every MCP permission request is checked again before approval; a restriction violation aborts the call like a file or shell violation. Unconfigured MCP servers are always rejected. MCP servers are trusted programs or remote services: restrict mcp controls which tools may be called, not their internal I/O. The fs, net and exec clauses do not sandbox an MCP server's implementation or its configured connection.","group":"The language"},{"title":"What is covered","page":"Restrictions","href":"restrictions.html#what-is-covered","text":"Caller Checked? --- --- Raw function calls ( read_file(...) , shell(...) ) yes Tool calls during explicit agent operations yes Generation ( ? / think ) No tools exposed; permission attempts abort the call. MCP tools from --mcp configuration and MCP scope checked Any other Copilot permission request rejected Plain SPL code (arithmetic, log , variables) nothing to restrict Future direction Raw tools and agents remain allow-by-default; a global deny-by-default mode is not implemented. Shell command inspection and trusted MCP services are not OS sandboxes.","group":"The language","signatures":["read_file(...)","shell(...)","agent","?","think","--mcp","log"]},{"title":"Failures & errors","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html","text":"Failures stop execution unless caught. Format, validation, transport, tool, and capability failures have distinct categories so recovery does not accidentally repeat external actions.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Failure payload","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#failure-payload","text":"line is 1-based and command contains the innermost failing statement's source line. Backend lifecycle checks outside a statement may use line zero and an empty command. Caught payloads are ordinary dict values.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Status codes","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#status-codes","text":"Code Cause --- --- invalid_format Malformed strict JSON, or a value that does not match the selected fmt type. validation_failed JSON parsed, but did not satisfy the schema. Reason includes field paths. runtime_error Invalid modifiers/schema, undeclared local assignment, undefined name, type/arity/index/arithmetic error. assertion_failed A business-rule assertion was false. explicit_failure The script executed fail. restriction_violation Blocked tool action, forbidden generation permission request, or record/replay capability violation. llm_error Authentication, timeout, transport, model, trace/recording I/O, or replay matching/completion failure. tool_error Missing file, HTTP failure, invalid tool input, or nonzero shell exit.","group":"The language","signatures":["invalid_format","validation_failed","runtime_error","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","llm_error","tool_error"]},{"title":"try / except","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#try--except","text":"The handler's name and variables declared in either branch belong to the same function/script scope. Guidance and restriction contexts unwind before the handler runs. Syntax errors occur before execution and cannot be caught. Runtime failures do not roll back prior writes or variable changes.","group":"The language"},{"title":"assert, fail, success","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#assert-fail-success","text":"","group":"The language"},{"title":"Assertions","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#assertions","text":"assert condition ['message'] checks a business rule independently of schema repair.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Explicit failure","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#explicit-failure","text":"fail reason stops with explicit_failure, unless caught.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Successful termination","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#successful-termination","text":"success [value] immediately ends the whole script, including from a function. return exits the current function, or the script when used at top level.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Bounded generation repair","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#bounded-generation-repair","text":"This permits one initial call plus two corrections. Only invalid JSON and schema mismatches use that budget. Each correction receives the previous answer and validation errors. Prompt/modifier expressions run once, and generation exposes no tools. Transport/model errors are not retried; external assertions are not repaired. Agents reject nonzero repair budgets. Do not retry entire side-effecting blocks blindly A raw write can finish before a later model call fails. Keep I/O outside repair, and design any manual action retry with idempotency and explicit error filtering. Neither try/except nor a variable store provides transactionality.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Uncaught failures and CLI errors","page":"Failures & errors","href":"failures.html#uncaught-failures-and-cli-errors","text":"An uncaught script failure prints its payload to stdout and exits with code 1. Syntax/usage errors go to stderr with code 2; internal errors use 70. Invalid recording files are usage errors. Replay mismatch/exhaustion is catchable, while unused entries are checked at backend close and still make the CLI fail before printing a success value. Opt-in variable stores save even after a script failure, including during workflow trace replay. Only legacy generation replay forbids stores and external writes. Workflow traces capture backend failures too, preserving their categories and reasons on replay. Logs, traces, and recordings can contain sensitive data.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Built-ins","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html","text":"A deliberately small, Python-flavoured standard library — enough to validate, reshape and report on LLM output without leaving the language. Semantics in one line An iterable is a list, a string (its characters) or a dict (its keys). Calling a built-in with the wrong number or type of arguments is a catchable runtime_error.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Special variables","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#special-variables","text":"Variable Description --- --- $env Read-only process-environment dict snapshot. $env.NAME or $env['NAME'] returns a string, or None if absent. Empty strings remain empty. $args Read-only list of strings from repeated CLI --arg options, in order; empty by default. Out-of-range integer indexes return None . Negative indexes count from the end. Both work in any scope and interpolation. Aliases remain read-only; assignments and mutating methods fail with runtime_error. Non-mutating dict/list operations work normally, and .copy() creates a mutable copy. The special names cannot be rebound. See runtime inputs and passing arguments.","group":"Reference","signatures":["$env","$args"]},{"title":"Functions","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#functions","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Raw tools: no model round-trip","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#raw-tools-no-model-round-trip","text":"read_file(path), write_file(path, content), web_fetch(url), web_search(query), and shell(command) are ordinary positional functions with required string arguments. They use active restrictions, return raw text (write returns confirmation), and never invoke a model. Shell returns combined stdout/stderr or raises tool_error on nonzero exit. Raw results are not truncated. See tool contracts. Use a separate generation expression to interpret output. Use explicit agent when the model should choose actions. Replay permits only read_file; all other raw tools are blocked.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Types & conversion","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#types--conversion","text":"Signature Description --- --- len(x) Length of a string (in characters), list or dict. str(x) Display form: strings as is, True / False / None , numbers plainly, lists and dicts as JSON. int(x) Truncates a number; converts True / False to 1/0; parses an integer string. float(x) Numbers pass through; booleans become 1/0; numeric strings are parsed. bool(x) Truthiness of any value. type(x) One of 'None' 'bool' 'number' 'str' 'list' 'function' 'dict' . list(x) A new list from any iterable.","group":"Reference","signatures":["len(x)","str(x)","int(x)","float(x)","bool(x)","type(x)","list(x)"]},{"title":"Numbers & sequences","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#numbers--sequences","text":"Signature Description --- --- range(stop) ; range(start, stop[, step]) List of integers; step may be negative but not zero. abs(n) Absolute value. round(n[, digits]) Without digits : rounds half to even ( round(2.5) is 2 ). With digits : rounds to that many decimal places. min(x) / max(x) ; min(a, b, …) Smallest / largest of an iterable or of the arguments. Empty input is an error. sum(x) Sum of an iterable of numbers. sorted(x[, reverse]) New sorted list. Pass a truthy second argument for descending order. reversed(x) New list in reverse order. enumerate(x) List of [index, item] pairs. zip(a, b, …) List of tuples-as-lists, as long as the shortest input. any(x) / all(x) True if any / every element is truthy.","group":"Reference","signatures":["range(stop)","range(start, stop[, step])","abs(n)","round(n[, digits])","min(x)","max(x)","min(a, b, …)","sum(x)","sorted(x[, reverse])","reversed(x)","enumerate(x)","zip(a, b, …)","any(x)","all(x)"]},{"title":"Dicts & JSON","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#dicts--json","text":"Signature Description --- --- keys(d) values(d) items(d) Lists of keys, values, or [key, value] pairs. json_parse(s) Parses a JSON string into a value. Invalid JSON raises runtime_error . json_stringify(v[, indent]) Serializes to JSON, optionally pretty-printed with indent spaces.","group":"Reference","signatures":["keys(d)","values(d)","items(d)","json_parse(s)","json_stringify(v[, indent])"]},{"title":"Logging","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#logging","text":"Signature Description --- --- log(a, b, …) ; log{level=debug}(a, b, …) Writes the str() of each argument, space-separated, to **stderr**. Plain calls default to info; the modifier selects error , info or debug , using a bare name, string or expression. The level is evaluated once and validated before arguments. Debug messages appear only at debug log level; silent suppresses all logs. Returns None . Never pollutes stdout.","group":"Reference","signatures":["log(a, b, …)","log{level=debug}(a, b, …)"]},{"title":"Methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#methods","text":"Methods are called with dot syntax on strings, lists and dicts. Strings are immutable; list and dict methods that say “in place” mutate the value and return None.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"String methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#string-methods","text":"Method Description --- --- upper() lower() Change case. title() capitalize() Title-case every word / capitalize the first character. strip() lstrip() rstrip() Trim whitespace from both / left / right. split([sep]) Without a separator: split on runs of whitespace. With one: split on exactly that text (empty separator is an error). splitlines() Split on \\n or \\r\\n . An empty string gives [] . join(items) ', '.join(parts) — every item must be a string. replace(old, new) Replace all occurrences. startswith(s) endswith(s) Prefix / suffix test. find(s) Index of the first occurrence, or -1 . count(s) Number of non-overlapping occurrences. isdigit() True for a non-empty string of digits 0-9 .","group":"Reference","signatures":["upper()","lower()","title()","capitalize()","strip()","lstrip()","rstrip()","split([sep])","splitlines()","join(items)","replace(old, new)","startswith(s)","endswith(s)","find(s)","count(s)","isdigit()"]},{"title":"List methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#list-methods","text":"Method Description --- --- append(x) Add an item at the end (in place). extend(iterable) Append all items of another iterable (in place). insert(i, x) Insert before index i (in place). pop([i]) Remove and return the item at i (default: last). remove(x) Remove the first item equal to x ; error if missing (in place). index(x) Index of the first equal item, or -1 . count(x) Number of equal items. sort([reverse]) reverse() Sort / reverse in place. copy() clear() Shallow copy / empty the list in place.","group":"Reference","signatures":["append(x)","extend(iterable)","insert(i, x)","pop([i])","remove(x)","index(x)","count(x)","sort([reverse])","reverse()","copy()","clear()"]},{"title":"Dict methods","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#dict-methods","text":"Method Description --- --- keys() values() items() Lists of keys, values, [key, value] pairs. get(key[, default]) Value for key , or default ( None if omitted). The safe alternative to d.key , which fails on a missing key. pop(key[, default]) Remove and return a key; error if missing and no default. update(other) Merge another dict in (in place). copy() clear() Shallow copy / remove all keys. Safe access to model output Models forget keys. Prefer r.get('label', 'unknown') or an explicit assert 'label' in r over r.label when you are validating parsed fmt='object' output.","group":"Reference","signatures":["keys()","values()","items()","get(key[, default])","pop(key[, default])","update(other)","copy()","clear()"]},{"title":"Putting it together","page":"Built-ins","href":"builtins.html#putting-it-together","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","text":"The interpreter runs one script file to completion. It prints the result on stdout, logs on stderr and reports the outcome through its exit code.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#usage","text":"From a checkout use node dist/cli.js or npx file:./lang. The package installs two equivalent binaries: spl and spl-lang. A script run expects exactly one script file; login and logout are separate commands, see Authentication.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Options","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#options","text":"Option Default Description --- --- --- --log-level \u003clevel> info error , info or debug . Logs go to stderr. --silent off Suppress all logs; only the result is printed. --model \u003cid> SDK default Overrides per-call model, then header model, then SDK default. --arg \u003cvalue> none Repeatable string argument exposed through $args , in order. Also accepts --arg=value . --mcp \u003cfile.json> none MCP servers in VS Code mcp.json format; see [MCP configuration](#mcp-configuration). --store \u003cfile.json> — Persist top-level variables in a JSON file. --store-module \u003cpath> — Custom store module exporting createStore() . Mutually exclusive with --store . --timeout \u003cseconds> 300 Per-LLM-call timeout. --trace \u003cfile.jsonl> none Create a new workflow trace of all LLM requests, responses, and failures, including agents. Works with --silent . --record \u003cfile.jsonl> none Create a new generation recording. Rejects existing files and agent calls. --replay \u003cfile.jsonl> none Replay recorded LLM/agent results without Copilot or MCP. Explicit script tools and stores run normally for workflow traces; legacy generation recordings retain their restrictions. Mutually exclusive with --trace and --record . --cwd \u003cdir> current dir Base directory for relative paths, restrict checks, and the working directory of tools and the model. --check off Parse the script and validate any --mcp config without starting servers or making LLM calls. -v , --version — Print the version. -h , --help — Print usage.","group":"Reference","signatures":["--log-level \u003clevel>","--silent","--model \u003cid>","--arg \u003cvalue>","--mcp \u003cfile.json>","--store \u003cfile.json>","--store-module \u003cpath>","--timeout \u003cseconds>","--trace \u003cfile.jsonl>","--record \u003cfile.jsonl>","--replay \u003cfile.jsonl>","--cwd \u003cdir>","--check","-v","--version","-h","--help"]},{"title":"Script arguments and environment","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#script-arguments-and-environment","text":"Options may appear before or after the single script file. Each --arg supplies exactly one string: the first is $args[0], the second $args[1], and so on. Empty values are preserved. Use --arg=--literal-option for strings beginning with -. Extra positional arguments are still usage errors; use --arg for each script argument. Without arguments $args is empty, and out-of-range indexes return None. $env.NAME or $env['NAME'] reads the process environment as a string, returning None for absent variables and '' for present-but-empty ones. Both special variables are read-only snapshots, including through aliases; they are not automatically persisted. They work during replay using the current process environment and supplied arguments. Neither is evaluated by --check. See runtime inputs.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Output streams & exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#output-streams--exit-codes","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"stdout","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#stdout","text":"The value of success or of a top-level return: strings raw, everything else as pretty-printed JSON. On an uncaught failure: the failure payload.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"stderr","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#stderr","text":"Logs, log() output, syntax errors, and for every LLM call the effective model and reasoning effort. Exit code Meaning --- --- 0 Success. 1 Uncaught script failure. 2 Syntax or usage error. 70 Internal error in the interpreter.","group":"Reference","signatures":["0","1","2","70"]},{"title":"Logging","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#logging","text":"Level Shows --- --- error Errors (for example a variable store that could not be saved), the full assembled prompt and raw response of every LLM call, and deterministic statement traces. info (default) Everything above plus warnings, one line per LLM call with its effective parameters and duration, think{show=True} reasoning, log() messages. debug Everything above plus tool invocations, caught failures and active restrictions. LLM requests and responses use the [llm] tag at every non-silent level. They may contain sensitive data; use --silent to suppress all logs. Each executed SPL statement is traced to stderr under [deterministic], with its line number, statement kind and source line, at every non-silent level. Loop bodies are traced for each iteration; skipped branches are not traced. This records control flow, including statements wrapping LLM calls, not intermediate expression values.","group":"Reference","signatures":["error","info","debug"]},{"title":"MCP configuration","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-configuration","text":"Pass a JSON file using the VS Code mcp.json shape: a top-level servers object maps server names to connection settings. MCP tools are available only during live agent calls. Generation and replay never start MCP servers. Workflow tracing supports agents; legacy generation recording rejects them.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Remote HTTP or SSE server","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#remote-http-or-sse-server","text":"For a URL-based server, explicitly set \"type\": \"http\" (or \"sse\" for an SSE endpoint). Omitting type defaults to stdio, so a config containing only url and headers is rejected. Keep credentials out of the JSON file. Set SOCIALTOAI_AUTHORIZATION in the environment before running SPL. Its value must be the complete header value required by the service (for example Bearer YOUR_TOKEN if that service requires Bearer authentication). SPL substitutes the value verbatim; it does not add an authentication scheme. Omit headers when the server does not require them.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Local stdio server","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#local-stdio-server","text":"A stdio server runs a local executable that speaks MCP over stdin/stdout. Replace the script path below with your MCP server's entry point. Keep the executable in command and its arguments as separate strings in args, not as one shell command.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Configuration fields","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#configuration-fields","text":"Server names use letters, digits, underscores, dots or hyphens. They are the names used in restrict mcp, not a server's URL or executable name. Field Applies to Meaning --- --- --- type All \"stdio\" (default), \"http\" or \"sse\" . Always specify it for remote servers. command stdio, required Non-empty string: executable name or path. args stdio, optional Array of argument strings, such as [\"server.js\", \"--port\", \"3000\"] . env stdio, optional Object mapping environment variable names to string values. cwd stdio, optional Working directory string; defaults to SPL's --cwd . Relative values resolve against --cwd . url http/sse, required Full MCP endpoint URL using http: or https: . headers http/sse, optional Object mapping HTTP header names to string values. tools All, optional Array of tool names exposed by the server, for example [\"search\"] . Omitted or [\"*\"] allows all; [] disables the server. This is a configuration allowlist, which restrict mcp can only narrow. timeout All, optional Positive number of milliseconds per MCP tool call, such as 30000 . This is separate from SPL's --timeout in seconds per LLM call.","group":"Reference","signatures":["type","command","args","env","cwd","url","headers","tools","timeout"]},{"title":"Variables, paths and unsupported settings","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#variables-paths-and-unsupported-settings","text":"${workspaceFolder} expands to SPL's --cwd, not the folder containing the JSON file. ${env:NAME} expands an environment variable in command, args, env, cwd, url or headers values. A missing variable is a configuration error, including during --check. The --mcp filename is relative to the invoking directory, independently of --cwd. The config may be anywhere; .vscode/mcp.json is a convention, not a requirement. Use strict JSON: no comments or trailing commas. Windows backslashes inside JSON strings must be escaped (\"C:\\\\tools\\\\server.js\"); forward slashes avoid this escaping. SPL supports the documented VS Code configuration subset, not every VS Code setting. Interactive inputs, ${input:...}, other variable forms, envFile and other unsupported server fields are rejected explicitly.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Validate, run and restrict access","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#validate-run-and-restrict-access","text":"With a script and config in the current directory: --check validates script syntax, JSON structure, settings and variable expansion without starting MCP servers or making LLM calls. It does not verify credentials, endpoint reachability, executable availability or tool names. Use server names from the JSON keys to limit tool access: restrict mcp 'socialtoai/tool_name' allows just one tool; replace tool_name with an actual tool exposed by that server. A tools list in the config uses unqualified names such as \"search\", not \"socialtoai/search\". Only load trusted configurations: they can launch local programs or connect to remote services. Server names and server/tool selectors in restrict mcp are case-sensitive. Without an MCP restriction all configured tools are allowed; no unconfigured MCP server is approved.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Variable store","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#variable-store","text":"Stores are independent of traces and recordings. Workflow trace replay loads and saves them normally. Replaying legacy --record files rejects both --store and --store-module before loading user modules or writing state. Scripts can keep state between runs. --store vars.json loads top-level variables from the file at start and writes them back at exit — also on failure. Functions are not persisted. The file must contain a JSON object; writes are atomic (temp file + rename).","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Custom stores","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#custom-stores","text":"--store-module ./mystore.mjs plugs in your own persistence. The module exports createStore() (or a default export of that shape) returning an object with load() and save(vars): Synchronous interface load() and save() are synchronous, so a store must complete its I/O before returning. createStore() itself may be async. save() receives plain JSON-compatible values (vars), never functions.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#authentication","text":"LLM calls go to GitHub Copilot, so the runtime needs credentials. Run spl login once: it does nothing if the runtime is already authenticated (an earlier login, a Copilot CLI or gh login, or COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN / GH_TOKEN / GITHUB_TOKEN); otherwise it starts GitHub's device flow, prints a one-time code and a URL, waits until you confirm in the browser, verifies the token with the Copilot runtime and saves it to ~/.spl/credentials.json (folder overridable with SPL_HOME). Scripts pick that token up automatically. spl logout deletes the saved file. A script that needs an LLM without credentials fails with llm_error and asks you to run spl login. Scripts that never call an LLM (and --check) need no login.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Backend","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#backend","text":"Each live call opens a fresh session through the GitHub Copilot SDK. Generation (? and think) exposes no tools and aborts unexpected permission requests. Only explicit agent sessions expose runtime and configured MCP tools under active restrictions. Pass context explicitly; previous calls are not implicit conversation history.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Workflow traces and replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#workflow-traces-and-replay","text":"--trace creates a new file and never overwrites an existing one. It instruments every backend call from ?, think, and agent, including repair attempts and failures that the script catches. Tracing is independent of log level and --silent. --trace, --record, and --replay are mutually exclusive. --check does not create a trace. Replay needs no model credentials and never constructs a Copilot client or starts MCP servers. It supplies the recorded raw response or failure at each LLM call. JSON parsing, schemas, repairs, assertions, and normal control flow run again. Requests must match exactly and in order: call kind, assembled prompt (including schema/guidance), requested model, reasoning effort, and show-reasoning flag. Changed inputs, exhaustion, and unused entries fail explicitly. Replay is not a dry run or a snapshot restore. Explicit script calls to read_file, write_file, shell, web_fetch, and web_search still execute under normal restrictions. Persistent stores also load and save normally, including after script failure. Environment variables, arguments, files, and explicit tool outputs come from the current run. Replay can therefore perform writes and network requests; those inputs must still produce matching LLM requests. Agent results are replayed without executing the agent or repeating its runtime tools or MCP calls. Only the completed agent call's request and result/failure are captured, not the SDK's internal conversation or individual tool exchanges. Replay does not recreate files or other side effects produced by the original agent; provision required state separately.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Trace format","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#trace-format","text":"The UTF-8 JSONL file begins with a versioned header, followed by one entry per completed backend call: Requests are structured objects; absent model/effort values are null. Responses preserve unparsed text, optional reasoning, and effective backend parameters. A failed call has error instead of response, for example: Backend errors replay as llm_error; runtime failures retain their original category and reason. Source locations are attached by the current script. Incomplete or malformed entries and unknown format versions are rejected explicitly. Protect traces Files contain assembled prompts and raw responses, including source data and reasoning. Treat them as sensitive; do not commit private traces. Trace files are created with owner-only permissions where supported by the operating system.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Offline generation record/replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#offline-generation-recordreplay","text":"This legacy mode remains supported with its original spl-generation, version 1 format. Record creates a new JSONL file and never overwrites one. Replay needs no credentials and never constructs a Copilot client or starts MCP servers. Both modes reject agents. Recording captures successful backend responses, including invalid JSON that subsequently triggers a repair; transport errors are not recorded. Requests must match exactly and in order: call kind, assembled prompt (including schema/guidance), requested model, reasoning effort, and show-reasoning flag. Changed prompts, exhausted recordings, and unused entries fail explicitly. Replay re-runs JSON parsing, schema validation, repair prompts, and deterministic code. Replay of these legacy files permits local reads only. Writes, shell, network, agents, and persistent stores fail before execution. Use --trace for workflow replay with explicit side effects and agent results. Protect recordings Files contain assembled prompts and raw responses, including source data. Treat them as sensitive; do not commit private recordings. Record mode still executes explicit raw I/O normally.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Reproducibility","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#reproducibility","text":"Control flow, arithmetic and interpolation are deterministic. For LLM calls reproducibility is best effort: The Copilot SDK has no temperature or seed setting, so the language has none either. The shared reasoning modifier maps to SDK effort; unsupported effort logs a warning and uses the model default. Use schemas, assertions, bounded generation repair, and offline record/replay to contain variance; see repair behavior.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Grammar (EBNF)","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html","text":"SPL 0.3 syntax, synchronized from lang/docs/syntax.ebnf. Modifier names are checked while parsing; expression values and schemas are checked at runtime.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Notation","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#notation","text":"{ x } means repetition, [ x ] means optional, and | separates alternatives. Statements end at a newline, semicolon, closing block, or end of input. Opening block braces must share the keyword's line. Comments begin with #.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Complete grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#complete-grammar","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Syntax versus semantics","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#syntax-versus-semantics","text":"let declares in the current block scope; reads and assignment search enclosing lexical scopes. Blocks and conditionals yield their final expression or None. Raw tools are ordinary function calls. Generation and agents share expression-valued modifiers; log has its own level modifier. See language basics and generation contracts for runtime rules. After changing the grammar or executable examples, update their Markdown copies in docs-template/. Run npm run docs:build from the repository root to regenerate all HTML pages and the search index. Documentation tests check that the embedded grammar and examples remain synchronized.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","text":"Start from a working script, adapt one prompt or guard, and run it again. These examples keep explicit I/O, LLM judgment, and acceptance checks together so you can scan the workflow and iterate without writing an orchestration layer. Scripts from lang/examples/ are synchronized with their executable sources. Most run from lang/; test repair runs against the npm project selected by --cwd. That option controls relative I/O and tool directories, not the script filename. Authenticate before live generation or agents. Use --model \u003cid> to override an unavailable example model. Default tests check every example and documentation snippet offline, including scripted triage and invoice runs. Authenticated Copilot tests require explicit SPL_LIVE_TESTS=1. Script Shows --- --- [ commit-message.spl ](#commit-message) shell under restrict exec 'git' , validation helper, retry loop [ mcp.spl ](#search-with-mcp) Explicit agent using a configured docs/search MCP tool [ triage-issues.spl ](#triage-issues) Schema-validated classification, bounded generation repair, aggregation [ extract-invoice.spl ](#extract-an-invoice) Structured extraction cross-checked with arithmetic before it is accepted [ code-review.spl ](#code-review-as-a-ci-gate) Three-file review, structured findings, restricted report write, fail as a CI gate [ release-notes.spl ](#release-notes) Nested forbid / enforce , git history to a file inside ./out [ web-digest.spl ](#web-digest) restrict net , web_fetch , catching a blocked second host [ fix-failing-tests.spl ](#fix-failing-tests) Raw npm test plus explicit repair agent, up to three rounds [ translate-docs.spl ](#translate-docs) Read once, generate translations, write exact output to script-chosen paths [ journal.spl ](#journal-with-persistent-state) State across runs with --store [ basics , hi , live , restrict-live , tools-live ](#feature-tours) Small feature tours","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Commit message","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#commit-message","text":"Read the staged diff with raw shell, restricted to git. Generation itself has no tools. A deterministic validator permits at most three message attempts; it never reruns the shell command. Pattern: deterministic validator The model proposes; is_valid() decides. The loop gives it three tries, and the final assert makes sure a script never succeeds with an invalid message.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Triage issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-issues","text":"Classify issues against a strict schema with enum labels/priorities and required reason text. Up to two repairs correct format/schema errors, without retrying transport errors. This generation-only example supports offline record/replay.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Extract an invoice","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-an-invoice","text":"Validate invoice shape with a nested schema and bounded repair, then independently check arithmetic with assertions. A business-rule failure is not automatically repaired.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Code review as a CI gate","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#code-review-as-a-ci-gate","text":"An explicit agent inspects source under filesystem and git restrictions, then tool-free generation structures its findings. The report write is restricted to ./reports, and high-severity findings fail the CI gate. A request to only review is guidance, not a read-only filesystem capability.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Release notes","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#release-notes","text":"Generate release notes from recent git history and save them to ./out/RELEASE_NOTES.md. The history-reading phase restricts executable names to git; the separate file-writing phase restricts filesystem access to ./out. Note how rules and restrictions nest.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Web digest","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#web-digest","text":"Summarize a web page with network access limited to a single host — and prove the sandbox holds by catching a blocked request to a second host.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Fix failing tests","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#fix-failing-tests","text":"Run raw npm test in the project selected by --cwd, invoke an explicit repair agent after a failure, and repeat up to three rounds. Files are restricted to the project and executable names to npm. Avoiding test edits is guidance, not a filesystem write prohibition. This workflow intentionally performs actions; review its changes. --trace captures its LLM calls, but replay reruns explicit tests without repeating the agent's edits, so it requires suitable external state. Legacy --record does not support this workflow. Pattern: failure as input A failing npm test is a tool_error whose reason contains the test output. Catching it turns the failure into the prompt for the next step.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Search with MCP","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#search-with-mcp","text":"Configure a trusted MCP server named docs exposing search, then run this explicit agent with --mcp. The exact docs/search selector narrows MCP access. Services are trusted; scopes limit calls, not their internal I/O. Generation-only calls never start MCP servers.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Translate docs","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#translate-docs","text":"Read the document once, generate German, Polish, and Japanese translations without tools, and write each exact response to a script-chosen path. Run from lang/ so the source document resolves correctly. No model is responsible for choosing or confirming the output path.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Journal with persistent state","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#journal-with-persistent-state","text":"A rolling journal that remembers previous runs through the variable store. Top-level variables are saved on exit and reloaded on the next run, so state survives between runs.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Feature tours","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#feature-tours","text":"Small scripts that exercise one area each.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"basics.spl — functions, objects and LLM calls","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#basicsspl--functions-objects-and-llm-calls","text":"Compare a computed Fibonacci number with an integer model response, then request a structured joke and a pet story. This tour includes LLM calls and requires authentication.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"hi.spl — compact end-to-end tour","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#hispl--compact-end-to-end-tour","text":"Use gpt-5.5 for a structured joke, reasoning and a greeting, then write the deterministic value fib(10) under ./out.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Live tours","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#live-tours","text":"live.spl — a quick smoke test of ? with len/fmt, think, enforce/forbid and assert against the real model. tools-live.spl — read_file, write_file and think{show=True} inside restrict './sandbox'. Run it after mkdir sandbox; echo hello world > sandbox/note.txt. restrict-live.spl — asks the model to read secret.txt outside the sandbox and shows the runtime blocking it, returning a restriction_violation payload.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html","text":"Syntax highlighting, snippets and editing support for .spl scripts. The extension lives in the vscode/ folder of the repository.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Features","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#features","text":"","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Highlighting","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#highlighting","text":"Keywords, let/def declarations, numbers, comments, the @{model=…} directive and strings with {expr} interpolation.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Generation & agents","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#generation--agents","text":"?, think, explicit agent, and shared modifiers: len, fmt, reasoning, show, schema, repairs, model. Raw tools use ordinary calls.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Runtime inputs","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#runtime-inputs","text":"Read-only $env and $args have highlighting, hover documentation and completions, including inside interpolation. Typing $ triggers completion; accepting $env or $args replaces the whole partial special-variable name without duplicating $. Diagnostics reject unknown special names and attempts to declare/rebind them. The editor never reads the process environment or script arguments. See runtime inputs.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Scopes & failures","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#scopes--failures","text":"enforce/forbid, restrict [fs|net|exec|mcp] with comma-separated clauses, try/except, assert, fail, success.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Snippets","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#snippets","text":"Type a prefix and press Tab. Covers control flow, LLM commands, tools and scopes.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Editing","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#editing","text":"# comments (Ctrl+/), bracket matching and auto-closing for {} [] () and quotes (including '''), auto-indent after {, #region folding.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Escape checking","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#escape-checking","text":"Illegal escapes and stray } in strings are flagged with the invalid scope. String highlighting understands interpolation {expr}, the {{ }} and \\{ \\} brace escapes, raw r'…' strings and triple-quoted strings. Lexical only The grammar is lexical (TextMate). spl --check rejects unknown/duplicate modifier names; expression values and schemas are validated at runtime. Navigation and completion respect lexical block scopes, including loop and exception bindings. Leveled log modifiers are highlighted and completed separately from generation modifiers.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Install","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#install","text":"For development, open the vscode/ folder in VS Code and press F5 to launch an Extension Development Host.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Snippets","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#snippets-1","text":"Type the prefix, then Tab. Area Prefixes --- --- Control flow def if ifelse for while let LLM ask askobj askschema think agent model Failures try retry (bounded schema repair) assert fail success Scopes enforce forbid restrict restrictnet restrictexec restrictmcp restrictmulti Tools read_file write_file web_fetch web_search shell","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Develop & test","page":"VS Code extension","href":"editor.html#develop--test","text":"test/*.spl contain scope assertions: a line # ^^^ scope.name asserts the scope of the columns under the carets on the line above (- scope asserts absence). test/run-assertions.mjs runs them with vscode-textmate. test/check-examples.mjs tokenizes every script in ../lang/examples and fails on invalid scopes or on strings/blocks that are left open at the end of a file. Keep the grammar in sync When the language changes, update syntaxes/spl.tmLanguage.json together with lang/docs/syntax.ebnf.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Architecture","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html","text":"A small tree-walking interpreter with explicit boundaries between raw I/O, generation, and agent actions.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Execution pipeline","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#execution-pipeline","text":"The lexer produces positioned tokens and string interpolation parts. The recursive-descent parser produces a typed AST and rejects invalid structure and unknown/duplicate modifier names. The interpreter evaluates modifier expressions, validates configuration, and runs the selected operation. Raw function call: evaluate arguments, check restrictions, run ToolHost, return exact output. Generation: evaluate prompt/options once, assemble guidance and schema, call a tool-free backend session, parse/validate output, optionally repair. Agent: create a session exposing runtime/configured MCP tools under the current restrictions; validate the final answer if requested, but never automatically retry actions.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Modules","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#modules","text":"Source Responsibility --- --- lexer.ts / parser.ts / ast.ts Tokenization, syntax validation, typed statements and expressions. interpreter.ts Lexical block scopes, expression-valued blocks/conditionals, leveled logging, control flow, raw built-ins, generation options, repair, scoped guidance/restrictions, source locations. values.ts / builtins.ts Value model, conversions, ordinary functions and collection methods. llm.ts Backend contract, prompt construction, strict JSON parsing. schema.ts Strict synchronous draft-07 schema compilation and field-path validation errors. copilot.ts Fresh SDK sessions, model/effort checks, agent-only tool exposure, permission enforcement. recording.ts Versioned workflow traces, legacy generation recordings, and exact sequential replay. tools.ts / restrict.ts File, network, search, shell, path canonicalization, allowlists, and intersecting restrictions. mcp.ts / auth.ts MCP configuration/filtering and Copilot device authentication. cli.ts / store.ts / logger.ts / errors.ts CLI lifecycle, opt-in variable persistence, logs, failures and termination.","group":"Guides","signatures":["lexer.ts / parser.ts / ast.ts","interpreter.ts","values.ts / builtins.ts","llm.ts","schema.ts","copilot.ts","recording.ts","tools.ts / restrict.ts","mcp.ts / auth.ts","cli.ts / store.ts / logger.ts / errors.ts"]},{"title":"Execution model","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#execution-model","text":"Each block creates a child environment; function calls link to their lexical closure rather than the caller. Reads and assignments search outward for the first declaration, while let declares/redeclares locally and shadows enclosing names. Each loop iteration and exception handler has its own bindings. Child declarations do not leak into parents or persistent globals. Block/conditional expressions yield the last expression or None; explicit control-flow signals still propagate normally. There is no declaration hoisting. Guidance and restrictions are execution context, not captured capabilities: a function called inside a policy block uses the caller's currently active policies. Policies unwind on return, success, or failure. Functions have a default recursion cap of 200. Failures receive the innermost statement location once. Return/break/continue propagate as control signals; success ends the entire script. Opt-in stores save globals even after script failure, including during workflow trace replay. Only legacy generation replay forbids stores and skips saving.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Generation and repair lifecycle","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#generation-and-repair-lifecycle","text":"Evaluate modifiers once and reject invalid values/combinations. Compile the schema before invoking the backend. Evaluate the prompt once; assemble guidance, restriction context, hints, and schema. Call a fresh generation session with no available tools or MCP servers. Parse strict JSON and validate the schema. Only format/schema failures may use the bounded repair budget. Agents use the same final-output validation but reject repairs. Custom backends must honor req.kind: only agent may invoke req.tools or other configured tools. The bundled Copilot backend enforces this through both tool exposure and permission callbacks.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#embedding","text":"run returns success with value and hasValue, or failure with its payload. Options include backend, logger, cwd, optional store, model override, args?: readonly string[], and maximum call depth. For example, run(parse('success $args[0]'), { backend, logger, args: ['first'] }) returns 'first'. Omitted args defaults to an empty list, not process.argv. Each run copies arguments and the current process.env into read-only $args and $env snapshots. Special-variable expressions bypass lexical bindings and stores. Missing environment keys and out-of-range argument indexes return None; ordinary collections keep their existing errors. Assignment and mutating-method checks preserve read-only behavior through aliases. The snapshots are not automatically persisted, but a script can explicitly bind their values or aliases to saved top-level variables. Other exports include authentication helpers, variable stores, MCP configuration helpers, and related TypeScript types.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Recording and replay","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#recording-and-replay","text":"Wrap a live backend with new TraceBackend(backend, file), or use new ReplayBackend(file) without constructing a live backend. Close either backend after use; replay checks for unused entries on close. Do not publish a successful result until that check completes. Traces contain structured requests and raw backend responses (including reasoning and effective parameters), or failures with their status code and reason. Every ?, think, and agent backend call is instrumented, including repairs and transport errors. Replay re-exercises parsing, schema checks, repairs, and deterministic code. Matching includes ordered assembled prompts, call kind, requested model, reasoning effort, and show-reasoning. See the JSONL format. The backend's mode identifies execution capabilities: trace records all LLM operations; workflow-replay returns their recorded results/failures while leaving explicit script tools and stores enabled. Agent-owned actions are not repeated or restored. No Copilot client or MCP server is started by replay. new RecordingBackend(backend, file) retains the legacy generation-only record mode. ReplayBackend selects replay for spl-generation files and workflow-replay for spl-trace files. Legacy replay rejects agents, stores, and all raw tools except local reads, preserving existing behavior.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Extension points","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#extension-points","text":"","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Custom backend","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#custom-backend","text":"Implement LlmBackend.call(req) returning text, optional reasoning, and effective-parameter metadata, plus close(). The request carries the operation kind, assembled prompt, requested model/effort, reasoning display flag, restriction snapshot, and ToolHost. Backend exceptions become script-level llm_error; runtime Failure values retain their category.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Custom store","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#custom-store","text":"Implement synchronous load() and save(vars), exported through a module factory. Stores are independent of model traces and are forbidden only in legacy generation replay mode.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Validation","page":"Architecture","href":"internals.html#validation","text":"The default suite is offline, using scripted backends and temporary files. Set SPL_LIVE_TESTS=1 explicitly to enable authenticated Copilot checks. Editor scope tests and documentation snippet tests cover the public language surfaces.","group":"Guides"}];
